translation:
Target is the proposition that for every type Fm of sentences and every type S of proof systems, equipped with propositional connectives (implication 🡒, conjunction ⋏, disjunction ⋎, negation ∼, truth ⊤, falsehood ⊥, with φ 🡘 ψ abbreviating (φ 🡒 ψ) ⋏ (ψ 🡒 φ)) and a provability relation ⊢ between systems and sentences; for every proof system 𝓢 of type S that satisfies classical propositional logic (i.e., proves every classical propositional tautology and is closed under modus ponens); for every three arbitrary functions T, K, D : Fm → Fm; and for every six sentences σ, S₀, S', G, P, Q in Fm, if all ten of the following hypotheses hold:
1. (Def6 𝓢 D T) For every sentence φ, 𝓢 proves D φ 🡒 (T φ 🡘 φ), i.e., D φ 🡒 ((T φ 🡒 φ) ⋏ (φ 🡒 T φ));
2. (Def5 𝓢 D K) For every sentence φ, if 𝓢 proves φ and 𝓢 proves D φ, then 𝓢 proves K φ (a meta-level rule, not an internal implication);
3. 𝓢 proves D σ;
4. 𝓢 proves D (σ 🡒 G);
5. 𝓢 proves σ 🡒 S₀;
6. 𝓢 proves S₀ 🡒 (T σ 🡒 S');
7. 𝓢 proves S' 🡒 G;
8. 𝓢 proves S' 🡒 ∼P;
9. 𝓢 proves Q 🡒 P;
10. 𝓢 proves σ 🡒 (K (σ 🡒 G) 🡒 Q);
then 𝓢 proves ∼σ.

Nothing else is required: 𝓢 is not assumed to be consistent or to satisfy any axioms or rules beyond classical propositional logic; T, K, and D are completely arbitrary functions on Fm with no assumptions beyond hypotheses 1 and 2 (none of Def1–Def4, Def7, or Def8 are assumed); and no arithmetic, Gödel numbering, or object-language quantifiers are assumed.

